viewwall (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Restart the wall when a feed stays wedged. A feed that connects, reports
    healthy and dies within seconds survives the branch rebuild viewwall
    already had for it; that case now fails the whole wall so the service
    manager gives it a clean process, instead of leaving one viewport black
    indefinitely. A camera that is merely off cannot trigger this: only a
    feed that reached healthy counts towards it.
  * Never give up on that restart, and spread repeated ones from five seconds
    out to a minute (systemd 254 or newer; older releases retry at a flat five
    seconds). A wall that keeps restarting still shows every healthy camera in
    between, where a stopped one shows nothing.
  * Reload the systemd unit on upgrade. The generated postinst appended its
    daemon-reload after the script's "exit 0", so it never ran and an upgrade
    kept the previously installed unit file. Upgrades from 0.1.2 and earlier
    need one "systemctl daemon-reload" by hand to pick this release up.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 19:09:56 -0400

viewwall (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Paint under the viewports so the framebuffer console no longer shows
    through the gaps, the outer margin, or a viewport whose feeds are all
    down. Configured with drm.background, which takes a hex colour or "none".
  * Add displays.<name>.mode, driving an output at a chosen resolution and
    letting the screen scale it back. Worth setting when the grid does not
    divide the native mode into tiles the size the cameras send.
  * Stop requiring displays.<name>.connector_id when only one display is
    configured, since there is nothing to disambiguate.
  * Report what each viewport actually presents. The metrics line gains
    presented_fps and sink_dropped, which count frames reaching the KMS plane
    rather than frames leaving the queue: on a nine viewport wall those differ
    by four to one.
  * Rename the queue side rate to queued_fps. The journal field VW_FPS is
    gone, replaced by VW_QUEUED_FPS and VW_PRESENTED_FPS; a saved journalctl
    query naming the old field needs updating.

 -- Sebastian Noack <sebastian.noack@gmail.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:55:56 -0400

viewwall (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Recover a feed whose branch wedges after repeated restarts, instead of
    leaving its viewport dark until viewwall is restarted by hand.
  * Report a stall watchdog whose timeout matches the startup default, which
    previously logged nothing and looked like a feed the scaling had missed.

